-- title:
--   One-sided pools of étale multisections on the ε-component
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of the extension $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ subject to the hypothesis `hK`, which identifies $K$ with [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), of the $q$-expansion field `x1FunctionField (M * p)` of $X_1(Mp)$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   The base is a discrete valuation ring $A$ which is a domain, equipped with an $A$-algebra structure on $L$ making $L$ the fraction field of $A$, such that the image of $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (hypothesis `hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (hypothesis `hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$. An element $j \in K$ is fixed whose image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the $j$-function (hypothesis `hj`), and $j$ is nonzero.
--
--   The scheme under consideration is $X =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229), the pushout gluing the two affine charts attached to $(A, K, j)$, with structure morphism $\pi =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ induced by the two chart algebras. An open subscheme $U \subseteq X$ is fixed such that the composite of the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ with $\pi$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$, and `hUmax` states that every open $W \subseteq X$ with this property satisfies $W \le U$; so $U$ is the smooth locus of $X$ over $A$.
--
--   Sections and the automorphism: $\varepsilon$ is an element of `SchemeHomOver (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of A))) π`, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} A \to X$ with $\varepsilon \circ \pi$ (in diagrammatic order, $\varepsilon$ followed by $\pi$) equal to the identity — a section of $X/A$ — and `hε` requires the set-theoretic image of $\varepsilon$ to lie in $U$. Further, $\sigma$ is an automorphism of $X$ as a scheme with $\sigma$ followed by $\pi$ equal to $\pi$ (hypothesis `hσ`), so $\sigma$ is an $A$-automorphism of the model, and $\varepsilon'$ is a second section with $\varepsilon$ followed by $\sigma$ equal to $\varepsilon'$ (hypothesis `hε'`).
--
--   The hypothesis `hfar` is a separation condition imposed on all non-smooth geometric fibres of all localisations $A_f =$ `Localization.Away f`, $f \in A$: for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A_f$, if the geometric fibre $X_{A_f} \times_{A_f} k \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ is not smooth, then, writing $U_s$ for the trace of $U$ on that fibre (the preimage of $U$ under the projection to $X_{A_f}$ followed by the projection to $X$) and $e$ for the geometric point of the fibre cut out by the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $A_f$ and then to $k$ (the image of the closed point of $k$ under `sectionFibrePoint (sectionBaseChange (Localization.Away f) ε) s`), two things hold: first, the map induced on the fibre by $\sigma$, namely `curveChange σ.hom hσ (specMap A (Localization.Away f))` pulled back along $s$, sends every point of the connected component of $U_s$ containing $e$ into $U_s$ minus that component; second, the corresponding geometric point attached to $\varepsilon'$ also lies in $U_s$ minus that component.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion asserts: for all natural numbers $A_0, B_0, n_0$ there exist $f \in A$ not lying in the maximal ideal of $A$, natural numbers $b$ and $M_b$ with
--   $$A_0 \, b^{\,n_0} + B_0 < M_b,$$
--   and the following data over $A_f =$ `Localization.Away f`.
--
--   First, a commutative ring $R'$ which is an $A$-algebra and an $A_f$-algebra compatibly (a scalar tower $A \to A_f \to R'$), module-finite, étale and faithfully flat over $A_f$.
--
--   Second, a family of commutative rings $B_i$, indexed by $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,M_b$, each a module-finite étale $A_f$-algebra, together with degrees $\deg i$ satisfying $1 \le \deg i \le b$ for every $i$, and $R'$-algebra isomorphisms
--   $$\varphi_i : R' \otimes_{A_f} B_i \;\simeq\; \mathrm{Fin}(\deg i) \to R',$$
--   so that $R'$ splits each $B_i$ completely into $\deg i$ copies of $R'$.
--
--   Third, morphisms $z_i : \operatorname{Spec} B_i \to X_{A_f}$, where $X_{A_f}$ is the fibre product of $\pi$ with $\operatorname{Spec} A_f \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, subject to the following conditions, one conjunct each: every $z_i$ is a closed immersion; every $z_i$ followed by the structure morphism $X_{A_f} \to \operatorname{Spec} A_f$ equals the morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B_i \to \operatorname{Spec} A_f$ induced by $A_f \to B_i$, i.e. $z_i$ is a morphism over $A_f$; the image of each $z_i$ is contained in the trace of $U$ on $X_{A_f}$ (the preimage of $U$ under the first projection); the images of the $z_i$ are pairwise disjoint; for every algebraically closed field $k$, every morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A_f$ and every $i$, the preimage of the image of $z_i$ under the projection from the geometric fibre $X_{A_f} \times_{A_f} k$ to $X_{A_f}$ is contained in the connected component, inside the trace of $U$ on that fibre, of the geometric point determined by the base change of $\varepsilon$; the image of each $z_i$ is disjoint from the image of the base-changed section `sectionBaseChange (Localization.Away f) ε`; the image of each $z_i$ is disjoint from the image of the base-changed section attached to $\varepsilon'$; and for all $i$ and $j$ (including $i = j$) the image of $z_i$ is disjoint from the image of $z_j$ followed by the automorphism of $X_{A_f}$ induced by $\sigma$, namely `curveChange σ.hom hσ (specMap A (Localization.Away f))`.
--
--   The final conjunct of the existential statement is `True`, so all the content lies in the existence of the data $f, b, M_b, R', (B_i), (\deg i), (\varphi_i), (z_i)$ with the listed properties.
--
--   This is the "one-sided pool" step in the construction of rational finite étale multisections on the smooth locus of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over a discrete valuation ring: it produces, after inverting a single element $f$ outside the maximal ideal, more than $A_0 b^{n_0} + B_0$ pairwise disjoint closed multisections of degree at most $b$, all confined on each geometric fibre to the connected component of the smooth locus carrying $\varepsilon$, and all avoiding $\varepsilon$, $\varepsilon'$ and the $\sigma$-translates. It is obtained from the construction of the modular unit in the finite chart, the associated family of level polynomials with étale quotients, the dictionary relating those quotients to components of the fibres, and the passage from level polynomials to pools; it is used in turn by [`ModularCurve.XOneP.exists_twoSidedPool_smoothLocus_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.exists_twoSidedPool_smoothLocus_twoChartModel_x1_mul), which merges two such pools.
